---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Feature from '../components/Feature.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Layout title="What's new" description="The changes of Civ 1.5 Remastered: unit health, healing, railroads, saves, Load Game, fixes.">
  <h1>What's new</h1>
  <p class="lead">The big changes first, the smaller fixes after them.</p>

  <Feature
    id="unit-health"
    title="Units have health"
    caption="Health bars over the units, all over the map."
    image="unit-health.png"
    secondCaption="A Chariot attacks a barbarian Legion that has already lost 2 hit points."
    secondImage="battles.png"
  >
    <p>
      A unit has 3 hit points, a veteran 4, and an elite unit 5. Damage stays after the battle, and a health bar
      shows it on the map and during the battle.
    </p>
    <p>
      Battles are fought in rounds: each round removes one hit point from the side that lost it. The original decided
      a battle with a single roll, so a Trireme could sink a Battleship; now a weak unit has to win several rounds in a
      row.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hit points are saved in a small file next to the saved game (<code>CIVIL0.HP</code> next to
      <code>CIVIL0.SVE</code>), so the original game can still load the save.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="load-game"
    title="Load Game in the game menu"
    caption="Load Game in the GAME menu."
    image="load-game.png"
  >
    <p>
      The game menu has <strong>Load Game</strong>, so you no longer need to quit to load a save. The load list shows
      the autosaves, newest first, or your own saved games.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="saves"
    title="10 save slots and 20 autosaves"
    caption="The 20 autosaves, one for every turn."
    image="saves.png"
    secondCaption="The 10 slots for your own saved games."
    secondImage="saved-games.png"
  >
    <p>
      Save your games in 10 slots (<code>CIVIL0</code> – <code>CIVIL9</code>) instead of 4. On top of that the game
      saves itself at every turn into 20 autosaves (<code>AUTO00</code> – <code>AUTO19</code>), the oldest one replaced
      first, so you can always go back up to 20 turns. The original had 4 autosaves, made only every 1000 years.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="wonders"
    title="Wonders never become obsolete"
    caption="Memphis with the Hanging Gardens, still making people happy."
    image="wonders.png"
  >
    <p>
      In the original most Wonders of the World stopped working once somebody discovered a later technology: the
      Hanging Gardens with Invention, the Pyramids with Communism, the Great Library with University. Now every Wonder
      works for as long as you hold its city.
    </p>
    <p>The Civilopedia describes the Wonders without the "until the development of" notes.</p>
  </Feature>

  <h2>Units</h2>

  <Feature id="elite-units" title="Elite units" caption="An elite unit after a won battle.">
    <p>A veteran that wins a battle becomes elite and gets a fifth hit point.</p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature id="healing" title="Healing" caption="Damaged units healing in a city.">
    <p>Units heal every turn:</p>
    <ul class="plain-list">
      <li>2 hit points in an own city, for every unit;</li>
      <li>1 hit point for a fortified land unit outside cities;</li>
      <li>1 hit point for a resting ship next to land, at most 3 tiles from an own city;</li>
      <li>aircraft heal only in cities.</li>
    </ul>
  </Feature>

  <h2>Moving around</h2>

  <Feature
    id="railroads"
    title="Railroads"
    caption="Railroads running into Thebes and on through the city."
    image="railroads.png"
  >
    <p>
      A city has a railroad once its owner knows Railroad. In the original a city only had a road, so a train lost a
      third of its moves in every city it crossed.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature id="goto-railroads" title="GoTo on railroads" caption="A GoTo order along a railroad.">
    <p>
      Moves along railroads are free also with GoTo and for the computer players. In the original only units moved by
      hand rode for free. Computer units get at most 30 free steps a turn, so nothing circles forever.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <h2>Help</h2>

  <Feature id="help-key" title="Alt+H always helps" caption="The help of the turn after Alt+H.">
    <p>
      Alt+H shows the help of the turn at any time. The original showed it only with the help option on and before
      the year 0.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <h2>Fixes of the original game</h2>

  <Feature
    id="demographics"
    title="Demographics"
    caption="The demographics of a large empire: approval and productivity counted right."
    image="demographics.png"
    secondCaption="That empire: cities of size 15 to 22, railroads everywhere, battleships at sea."
    secondImage="large-empire.png"
    wide
  >
    <p>
      The demographics screen counted with 16-bit numbers and overflowed in large empires, showing negative approval,
      productivity and disease. It now counts correctly.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature id="pillage" title="Pillage" caption="A unit pillaging next to a foreign city.">
    <p>Pillaging next to a foreign city no longer corrupts the game's memory.</p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature id="random-numbers" title="Better random numbers">
    <p>
      The random numbers come from xoshiro128**, a fast modern generator, instead of the weak generator of the
      original.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <h2>Smaller fixes</h2>
  <ul class="plain-list">
    <li>The palace style preview is drawn correctly.</li>
    <li>The game no longer freezes after a mouse click, and no longer crashes at the end of a turn.</li>
    <li>The arrow keys work in menus.</li>
    <li>Correct palette and screen in the window.</li>
    <li>The title logo, a new building in a city, the palace and the replay dissolve onto the screen again.</li>
    <li>The original AdLib and Roland MT-32 music, played through an emulated sound card and General MIDI.</li>
    <li>The game starts in a maximized window; F11 switches to full screen.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Fixes of places where the C# translation behaved differently from the DOS game are listed
    <a href={`${base}/translation-fixes/`}>on a separate page</a>.
  </p>
</Layout>
